This is a clever two-stage approach, but whether it's valid depends on what you're trying to accomplish. Let me walk through the concerns:
The main conceptual issue: Target leakage
Your course rankings come from a model trained on completers only. When you use these rankings to predict withdrawal, you're essentially saying: "I'll predict withdrawal based on how well students who didn't withdraw performed in these courses."
This creates a subtle circularity: withdrawers are likely systematically different from completers (that's why they withdrew), so the counterfactual grades you're predicting for them might be systematically biased.
When this might still be reasonable:

Descriptive analysis: If you're trying to understand "Do students tend to withdraw from courses where they would have performed poorly?", this is a valid exploratory approach. You're testing a hypothesis about selection.
Course difficulty proxy: If you think the ranking captures something about student-course fit that your other features don't (like "this student would struggle in proof-based courses"), it could add predictive value despite the leakage concern.
Late-semester prediction: If you're predicting withdrawal late in the semester when you have midterm grades or other performance indicators, the leakage is less problematic because you have contemporaneous performance data.

Alternative approaches to consider:

Instead of individual counterfactual rankings, use course-level difficulty measures (average grade, withdrawal rate, etc.)
Include "distance from typical successful student profile" for each course
Use the first model's predicted grade directly as a feature (rather than just the ranking)

